.TH BUILD 8
.SH NAME
build \- the build driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build --
[
.I target
] [
.I option ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B build.odin
compiles Vectra, stages a bootable disk, and starts it under QEMU.
It is an Odin program rather than a shell script, so the flag handling,
the per-architecture table and the link line grow in the same language
and the same type system as the thing they build.
.B justfile
and
.B Makefile
are thin wrappers over it, named
.BR kernel ", " esp ", " run ", " gui ", " debug ", " gdb ", " release ", " clean ", " font
and
.BR check ,
for a shell without either.
This page describes the driver itself.
.PP
The
.B \-out:
is not cosmetic.
Without it,
.B odin run
names the driver binary after the script and drops
.B ./build
directly on top of the
.B build/
output directory, destroying what the previous target staged there.
.SS Targets
.TP 10
.B kernel
Compile and link
.B kernel/
into
.BR build/vectra.elf .
The default target.
.TP
.B user
Compile the ring 3 programs into
.BR build/user .
.TP
.B programs
Compile the ring 3 test programs into
.BR build/programs .
.TP
.B check
Type-check the kernel and every program for one architecture, without
linking.
.TP
.B esp
Stage a bootable EFI system partition in
.BR build/esp .
.TP
.B run
.BR esp ,
then boot it under QEMU.
.TP
.B debug
.BR run ,
but halted and waiting for
.I gdb
on
.BR :1234 .
.TP
.B clean
Remove
.BR build/ .
.TP
.B lint
Check the tree's prose against ASD-STE100.
.PD
.SS Options
.TP 20
.BI \-\-arch= arch
.BR amd64 ,
.B arm64
or
.BR riscv64 .
Selects the architecture for every target.
Default
.BR amd64 .
.TP
.B \-\-release
Optimise, with bounds checks off.
Without it every build is a debug build.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial= where
.B stdio
or
.BR file .
Where QEMU's COM1 goes.
Default
.BR stdio .
.B file
writes
.BR build/serial.log .
.TP
.BI \-\-monitor= path
Open a QEMU monitor on a Unix socket at
.IR path ,
for
.B screendump
and the like.
Off unless given.
.TP
.B \-\-gfx
Open a QEMU window.
Without it QEMU runs headless, with
.B \-display none
-- the framebuffer is still real, only not shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-smp= n
Cores QEMU presents.
Default 4.
.B \-\-smp=1
is the uniprocessor control: the same boot, with one core brought up
and none of the multicore checks run.
.PD
.SS The toolchain
.BR odin ,
.BR clang ,
.BR ld.lld ,
a
.B qemu-system-
binary for the target architecture, and, only to regenerate the
console font, Python 3 with Pillow.
.I Clang
assembles the architecture's
.B .S
files.
Its
.B \-target
names the ELF the kernel links for and not the host clang runs on,
which is what lets a host's clang assemble for a freestanding kernel
at all.
.PP
UEFI firmware is QEMU's.
On amd64, a combined OVMF image is used when the build finds one beside
the tree.
Otherwise the split code and variable-store images that ship beside
the QEMU install are searched for under
.BR /opt/homebrew/share/qemu ,
.B /usr/local/share/qemu
and
.BR /usr/share/qemu ,
in that order, and the driver stops with an error if none is found.
.SS What gets staged
.B esp
copies the architecture's Limine executable and
.B boot/limine.conf
under
.BR build/esp/EFI/BOOT ,
and the linked kernel to
.BR build/esp/vectra.elf .
It then stages every program named in the driver's
.B user_programs
table: each compiled image goes to
.BR build/esp/vectra/bin ,
under the name the table gives it.
.B apps/rc/rcmain
and
.B apps/rc/init
go to
.BR build/esp/vectra/lib ,
along with the draw server's
.B keys
and
.B workspaces
files and
.BR tests/tools.rc ,
staged under
.BR build/esp/vectra/lib/tests .
.B build/esp/vectra/tmp
is removed and remade empty on every build, because what a previous
run wrote there was that run's own proof.
.PP
A program is added to the build by adding one entry to
.B user_programs
in
.BR build.odin :
a name and the package directory that builds it.
The next
.B esp
or
.B run
compiles it and stages it under its name in
.BR /bin ,
with no other change needed.
.SS Debugging the kernel
.B just debug
(or the
.B debug
target directly) boots QEMU halted, with
.B \-s
and
.BR \-S ,
waiting for a debugger on
.BR localhost:1234 .
There is no
.I gdb
on this machine.
.I Lldb
attaches with symbols and line numbers:
.IP
.EX
lldb build/vectra.elf -o 'gdb-remote localhost:1234'
.EE
.PP
QEMU's
.B \-s
opens the same stub even on an ordinary
.BR run ,
without halting, so a boot that loops can be read the same way,
mid-loop.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
just run
just gui
just debug
just release
just check
odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build -- run --gfx
odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build -- run --arch=arm64 --serial=file
odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build -- check --arch=riscv64
odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build -- lint --show
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B build/vectra.elf
the linked kernel
.TP
.B build/esp
the staged EFI system partition
.TP
.B build/disk.img
the scratch disk, made once and kept
.TP
.B build/serial.log
QEMU's COM1, with
.B \-\-serial=file
.TP
.B build/user
.TP
.B build/programs
compiled ring 3 images, before staging
.SH SOURCE
.B build.odin
.br
.B justfile
.br
.B Makefile
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR boot (8),
.IR verify (8),
.IR lint (8),
.IR genfont (8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An unrecognised target, an unrecognised
.BR \-\-arch= ,
or an
.B \-\-smp=
below 1 stops the driver with a message naming what it wanted.
A missing firmware image stops it before QEMU is started.
